<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-distributivity-of-set-operations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Distributivity of set operations
  </title>
  
  <idx>
    Distributive property
  </idx>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> S \cap \of{ T \cup U } = \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U } </m> for all sets <m> S </m>, <m> T </m>, and <m> U </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> x \in S \cap \of{ T \cup U } </m>. Then <m> x \in S </m> and <m> x \in T \cup U </m>, and so <m> x \in T </m> or <m> x \in U </m>. If <m> x \in T </m>, then <m> x \in S \cap T </m>, and so <m> x \in \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U } </m>. On the other hand, if <m> x \in U </m>, then <m> x \in S \cap U </m>, and so <m> x \in \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> x \in \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U } </m> for all elements <m> x \in S \cap \of{ T \cup U } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          S \cap \of{ T \cup U } \subseteq \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U }
        </md>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now let <m> x \in \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U } </m>. Then <m> x \in S \cap T </m> or <m> x \in S \cap U </m>. If <m> x \in S \cap T </m>, then <m> x \in S </m> and <m> x \in T </m>. In particular, <m> x \in T \cup U </m>, and so <m> x \in S \cap \of{ T \cup U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> x \in S \cap \of{ T \cup U } </m> for all <m> x \in \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U } \subseteq S \cap \of{ T \cup U }
        </md>;
        
        we now conclude that <m> S \cap \of{ T \cup U } = \of{ S \cap T } \cup \of{ S \cap U } </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> S \cup \of{ T \cap U } = \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U } </m> for all sets <m> S </m>, <m> T </m>, and <m> U </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> x \in S \cup \of{ T \cap U } </m>. Then <m> x \in S </m> or <m> x \in T \cap U </m>. If <m> x \in S </m>, then <m> x \in S \cup T </m> and <m> x \in S \cup U </m>, and so <m> x \in \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U } </m>. On the other hand, if <m> x \in T \cap U </m>, then <m> x \in T </m> and <m> x \in U </m>. In this case, <m> x \in S \cup T </m> and <m> x \in S \cup U </m>, and so <m> x \in \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> x \in \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U } </m> for all elements <m> x \in S \cup \of{ T \cap U } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          S \cup \of{ T \cap U } \subseteq \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U }
        </md>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now let <m> x \in \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U } </m>. Then <m> x \in S \cup T </m> and <m> x \in S \cup U </m>. In particular, if <m> x \notin S </m>, then <m> x \in T </m> and <m> x \in U </m>, and so <m> x \in T \cap U </m>. We conclude that <m> x \in S \cup \of{ T \cap U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> x \in S \cup \of{ T \cap U } </m> for all <m> x \in \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U } \subseteq S \cup \of{ T \cap U }
        </md>;
        
        we now conclude that <m> S \cup \of{ T \cap U } = \of{ S \cup T } \cap \of{ S \cup U } </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <conclusion>
    <p>
      Taken together, these results are called the <term> distributive properties </term> of the set operations.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</exercise>
